Problems with existing software
-------------------------------
Most of  the problems with  today's software  stem from  the poor management of 
data.  Data related  errors  such  as  dangling  pointers may stay in your code
unnoticed for a long time,  and are often difficult  to  find.   There  are  no
generic, fully typed libraries for data structures in C, and standard C++ class
libraries based  on  the NIH model are not suitable for routine commercial use,
because they require too much manual interaction.  They are also inefficient at
run-time (see illustration, next page). Some libraries offer persistent objects,
but require custom coded IO functions for every new class.

Code Farms' solution
--------------------
We think that data structures should be as easy to use as strcmp()  or qsort(),
and we  provide  a  transparent layer which acts as if the compiler handles the
data structures automatically.  For example,   assume  that you want to write a
program  for  processing  data  related to a Company  that  includes  Projects,
Departments,  and Employees.  You start by declaring a class/structure for each
object type, but without using the usual pointers that express relations between
objects.  Then you describe the  organization  separately,   in  several simple
statements:

        AGGREGATE(depts,Company,Department);
        AGGREGATE(byDept,Department,Employee);
        AGGREGATE(byProj,Project,Employee);
        AGGREGATE(byDate,Company,Project);
        AGGREGATE(byRev,Company,Project);

Booch's diagram, when using boxes instead of "clouds", looks like this:

          ............................
          :     C o m p a n y        :
          :..........................:
           1      1              1
           |      |              |
    byDate |      | byRev        | depts
           |      |              |
           n      n              n
  ....................       ..........................
  :  P R O J E C T   :       :  D E P A R T M E N T   :
  :..................:       :........................:
              1                  1
              |                  |
              | byProj           | byDept
              |                  |
              n                  n
          ............................
          :     E m p l o y e e      :
          :..........................:

 
Here AGGREGATE is one of many data organizations available from the library. For
example,  the first line says that any Company may contain several Departments.
The relation has  its  name,  depts.  In the same simple way,  you can invoke a
LINKED_LIST, GRAPH, TREE, HASH_TABLE, DYNAMIC_ARRAY, Lisp-like PROPERTY, M_TO_N
entity-relationship, or any other data structure.  The library is open, you can
modify it or add new features.  It also  supports memory management,  paging to
disk, free lists, blasting of memory to disk, and portable data formats.

The Library
-----------
The library includes a special code generator, which runs very fast - typically
requires less than  1/10 the compilation time.  It reads  the data declarations
and connects your program  to a special library.  It also  inserts the required
pointers into your classes/structures.  This happens automatically and transpa-
rently.  You don't  know about these pointers and you don't have to worry about
debugging them.

The code generator  also  creates special interface classes,  one for each data
organization,  deposits them into the file zzincl.h,  and then it moves all the
functions you will need into the file zzfunc.c.  Since the code generator knows
the locations of all  the pointers,  it can  generate all the IO functions that
make the data persistent. You simply compile and debug our original source with
those two files:


     C++:   #include "zzincl.h"
            //  your class/structure declarations
            //  data declarations (AGGREGATES etc)
            int main(void){
                Project *p; Employee *e; Department *d;
                  ...
                byProj.add(p,e); // add e under p
                byDept.del(e); // delete e from its dept
                byDept_iterator it(d); // prepare a loop
                while(e=it++){ // traverse Employees in d
                    // .. do something with e
                }
                ...
            }
            #include "zzfunc.c"

       C:   #include "zzincl.h"
            /*  your class/structure declarations */
            /*  data declarations (AGGREGATES etc) */
            int main(void){
            Project *p; Employee *e; Department *d;
               ...
            ZZ_ADD(byProj,p,e); /* add e under p */
            ZZ_DEL(byDept,d,e); /* delete e from d */
            /* next loop traverses all e under d */
            ZZ_A_TRAVERSE(byDept,d,e){
                /* .. do something with e */
            }ZZ_A_END;
            }
            #include "zzfunc.c"


Better than templates
---------------------
Using  the  library  is  as easy as  using  templates  but,   internally,   the
implementation is different. Code Farms' approach is more flexible,  automatic,
run-time  efficient,   and because it involves fewer objects,  it is easier  to
maintain. (Illustration comparing the two approaches is available on request,it
cannot be reproduced without  graphics.)   The  whole  concept  is more object-
oriented than in other class libraries, because it represents data relations as
objects. Associations and aggregations map directly into the library. Note that
the code generator is not the usual preprocessor,it does not modify your source.
You run it only once, after you declare your data. The library also protects the
integrity of your data.  If you add an object to  the  wrong organization,  the
compiler finds  the  error,   and  if you create invalid pointers,  the library
detects it at run-time. The C++ version is fully encapsulated, the C version is
based on protected macros.

Database Maker
--------------
In minutes,  you can set up a  fast  memory-resident  database  based  on  data
structures,  without the limitations of a database language,  such as SQL.  The
database becomes a part of your code, and is so fast that is can be used inside
algorithmic loops without loss of efficiency. Schema migration is supported.

